#figure(image("figures/retrieval_js.png", width: 100%), caption: [Retrieval on five held-out JavaScript repositories (mean with 95% bootstrap interval; higher is better). BM25 wins on locate; the graph ranker wins on dependency; fusion helps on impact but not beyond the graph ranker.])

#figure(image("figures/retrieval_py.png", width: 100%), caption: [The same comparison on four Python repositories. The pattern matches JavaScript.])

*Locate.* BM25 is the best ranker on all three groups. On the held-out JavaScript repositories the hybrid's MRR is 0.280 below BM25's (95% CI \[−0.373, −0.188\], p < 0.0001); on Python it is 0.167 below (\[−0.232, −0.103\]). Fusing BM25 into the hybrid recovers part of the gap but not all of it (held-out −0.168, p = 0.0003). The hybrid beats the vector ranker alone on the post-hoc JavaScript (+0.078, p = 0.009) and Python sets (+0.104, p = 0.003) but not on the held-out set (+0.020, \[−0.082, 0.120\], p = 0.71).

*Dependency.* The structural ranker is best: on held-out repositories MRR is 0.974 against 0.640 for BM25 (+0.333, p < 0.0001). The hybrid beats BM25 and the vector ranker by wide margins but is not better than the graph ranker it contains (−0.059, \[−0.134, 0.010\], p = 0.14). This category is nearly a lookup in the import graph, and the oracle derives importers from the same relation, so the graph ranker's score largely measures analyzer accuracy (Section 5.3), not retrieval skill.

*Impact.* The graph ranker clearly beats text rankers (held-out nDCG\@10 0.744 against 0.557 for BM25; post-hoc 0.907 against 0.416; Python 0.938 against 0.431). The hybrid improves on the graph ranker on the held-out set in recall (+0.141, \[0.027, 0.277\], p = 0.032) but not in nDCG (+0.106, \[−0.003, 0.234\], p = 0.092), and it is indistinguishable from it on the post-hoc (−0.019, p = 0.074) and Python (−0.011, p = 0.14) sets.

*Summary.* For structural questions a structural ranker is needed; fusion beats the lexical and dense rankers on them only because it contains the structural one, and it does not beat that ranker alone. For location questions none of the semantic or structural rankers, alone or fused, beats BM25, and the vector ranker (latent semantic analysis, not a neural model) is weaker than BM25 throughout.

== 5.2 Graph Path Pruning (RQ2)

#tbl([Execution-flow reconstruction on the 25 held-out flows. A flow question asks which file-to-file dependency edges carry a behaviour. "Edges returned" is the average number of edges a method outputs; the ideal answer has two or three. The best F1 is bold on green.])[
  #flows-held-out
]

#figure(image("figures/flows.png", width: 100%), caption: [Execution-flow reconstruction on the 25 held-out flows. Left: F1 with 95% bootstrap interval. Right: edges returned per question. Pruning shrinks the output by about 88%, but only the post-hoc hybrid variant (hatched) is above the simple top-3 baselines, and not significantly.])

*Fan-out.* The primary pruner returns 87.8% fewer edges than unpruned BFS (1.9 against 15.7 per question). Its F1 is higher than BFS (+0.119, \[−0.009, 0.259\], p = 0.097) but the difference is not significant at the 0.05 level.

*Against simple baselines.* The primary pruner is below the top-3-by-path-words baseline (−0.100, \[−0.280, 0.079\], p = 0.30) and below the top-3-by-BM25 baseline (−0.056, p = 0.31); neither difference is significant, and neither comparison supports the pruner. On the four JavaScript library questions the primary variant returned no edge at all, because reciprocal-rank relevance is smaller than path-word overlap and few edges reach the fixed threshold; this is a defect of the scoring scale, left unfixed so the held-out result stays untouched.

*The role hierarchy and continuity components.* Switching off the role hierarchy changes nothing on the held-out set (difference 0.000): the benchmark repositories are mostly libraries, not layered route/controller/service applications. Switching off the continuity bonus improves F1 (0.264 against 0.200, p = 0.125). We therefore cannot claim that either component helps.

*Fused relevance (exploratory).* Taking file relevance from the fused BM25/vector/graph ranking gives the best F1 (0.353): +0.272 over BFS (\[0.161, 0.380\], p = 0.0001) and +0.097 over the BM25-relevance top-3 baseline (\[0.023, 0.177\], p = 0.031), but only +0.053 over the path-words baseline (\[−0.091, 0.197\], p = 0.50). This variant was chosen after seeing the held-out results of the others, so it needs a fresh set to count as confirmation. On the 23 dev flows it ties the path-words baseline (0.325 each).

== 5.3 Multi-language analysis (RQ3)

#tbl([How well the analyzer reproduces independent reference tools. "Found" is the share of the reference items the analyzer also reports; "correct when reported" is the share of what it reports that the reference confirms.])[
  #accuracy
]

#figure(image("figures/accuracy.png", width: 100%), caption: [Import-edge recall per repository against the independent oracles. The vertical axis starts at 0.85 to make differences visible.])

Per Python repository, import recall is 0.922 (`requests`), 0.983 (`click`), 0.963 (`flask`) and 1.000 (`httpx`). The analyzer's first Python resolver searched only ancestor directories for package roots and recovered 0.637 of the pairs; it missed imports of a package that lives in a sibling source tree, such as `import click` in `examples/` when the package is at `src/click`. We added a dotted-path suffix fallback after seeing this comparison, so the 0.968 figure is not a blind result. Remaining JavaScript misses are mostly `export … from` re-exports, which the graph records as exports. Analysis took 0.1 s to 5.4 s per repository, and no repository failed.

*Python retrieval.* The findings match those on JavaScript (Table 2): BM25 best for locate (MRR 0.851 against 0.683 for the hybrid), the graph ranker best for dependency (0.949) and impact (nDCG\@10 0.938), and the hybrid no better than the graph ranker for impact (−0.011, p = 0.14).

= 6. Threats to validity and limitations

+ *Oracle overlap.* Dependency and impact gold derives from import relations, the same information the graph holds. High structural scores measure analyzer accuracy and the value of the relation, not a hard retrieval problem.
+ *Locate favours text.* Queries are documentation sentences; they reward matching words in the file. Open-ended conceptual questions are not covered.
+ *Latent semantic analysis is not a neural embedding.* The "semantic" ranker is weak. A pretrained code-embedding model could change the vector and hybrid rows; we did not test one.
+ *Flow benchmark is small and annotated by the authors.* Forty-eight questions, two to three gold edges each, seven repositories with library-style structure. The annotator also wrote the pruner. Independent re-annotation is needed.
+ *Post-hoc choices.* The content-based pruner was designed after error analysis on the dev set; the hybrid-relevance variant after the held-out run; the Python suffix fallback after an accuracy comparison. Each is flagged where it appears.
+ *Pruner constants.* Thresholds and weights were fixed in advance and not tuned, which exposed a scale mismatch (empty outputs). They are not optimised.
+ *Omitted from this report.* A language-model-only baseline and a function-relationship category (callers and callees) were not built; no language model answers were evaluated; there is no user study.
+ *Statistics.* Many comparisons, no correction; samples of 25 to 145 questions.

= 7. Conclusion

The retrieval stack is implemented and measured. Structure is indispensable for dependency and impact questions and text search is hard to beat for location questions; RRF fusion of the semantic and structural rankers does not improve on the better component for either. Graph Path Pruning shrinks flow output by about 88% but, in its pre-declared form, did not beat a top-3 baseline on held-out flows. A variant that scores edges by fused-ranking relevance looks promising (F1 0.353) and needs confirmation on new questions. Python support matches the standard library on definitions and recovers 96.8% of imports. Future work: a neural embedding model, an independent annotation of flows, a scale-aware pruning threshold, and end-to-end tests with a language model and users.

